"""Authored research notes; no changes to the frozen transcription."""
from work import *
NOTES=[]
def N(scan,suffix,title,kind,locus,body,context,ids,check='carried as background without fault found',outcome='',confidence=''):
 NOTES.append(dict(id=f'SP31-N{scan:03}-{suffix:02}',scan=scan,title=title,kind=kind,locus=locus,body=body,context=context,ids=ids,check=check,outcome=outcome,confidence=confidence))
N(2,1,'A new axiomatics, with a bounded objective','conjecture','opening and data (a)',
 '“Je voudrais définir une autre axiomatique” announces a replacement presentation of networks on a set of lieux: moderate pairs are primitive, and finite moderation is defined pairwise. This adds a concrete cut-based test case to the Esquisse’s program of reconstructing stratified objects from pieces, but the present page does not define a general form or give a reconstruction equivalence. S14 is therefore motivated here, not established.',
 'E 119/27,32–34: pièces de construction; axiomatique; provisional systems. S §1.1 restates the current proposal.', ['WG2 B15 (§1.2)','MPL_150_044-084 Q9','S14 (packet README)'])
N(2,2,'The finite moderate sets form a flag complex','own idea','definition of finite moderate T',
 'The condition “P₂(T)⊂S₀” makes the finite moderate subsets exactly the cliques of the graph with vertex set L and edge set S₀, so they form an abstract simplicial complex determined by its edges. This is a useful finite combinatorial organization of the input, but supplies neither the complement partitions nor the attachment maps needed for geometric reconstruction. In particular it should not be identified with the flag-indexed spaces of the Esquisse merely because both constructions use finite flags.',
 'E 119/36–37,58–59: Drap(I), pair tubes, and additional composition data.', ['WG2 §3.1 #2','STRATA A.4','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'], 'targeted proof check','Every subset of a clique is a clique; the edge relation alone specifies the finite moderate-set complex. No realization theorem checked.','High for the combinatorial statement: it is an immediate consequence of the displayed pairwise definition; no novelty claim.')
N(2,3,'GF III in the frozen text versus CF III in the context','reading question','upper-right enclosed annotation',
 'The frozen transcription reads “GF III,” whereas S’s title and later cross-reference call this part “CF III.” That discrepancy is an editorial reading question, not permission to change the now-frozen letter; the G reading was based on the inward foot visible in the enlarged annotation. The later page 156-7/071 is not among the present images, so the context supplies a nomenclatural lead only.',
 'S title, §1.7 and §3; STUDY §4 and appended 156-3 reading; later 156-7/071 is reported there.', ['S14 (packet README)'],outcome='Post-freeze contextual discrepancy recorded; no TeX change and no later image inspected.')
N(3,1,'The complement-recovery question is answered in the margin, but not safely proved','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','long oblique margin; lower NB',
 '“Question : T est-il connu quand on … C_L(T) ?” is followed by “l’inverse est-il vrai ? Oui” and a short attempted proof, then repeated as an affirmative NB. The proof needs a point in T′ outside T to be compatible with every point of T, a condition not supplied merely by T and T′ each being moderate. Thus this is a question the manuscript locally answers, whose answer must still be tested rather than silently treated as an open question or a valid theorem.',
 'S §1.1 explicitly notices the missing compatibility; E 119/28–29 contrasts primitive pieces with actual geometric complements.', ['PATHS E.10','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'], 'targeted proof check','Checked the logical use of membership in C(T); individual moderation of T and T′ does not imply their union is moderate.')
N(3,2,'A valid tronçon with two different singleton cuts having the same complement','own idea','C(T) definition and affirmative margin',
 'Take L=Q×{0,1}, ordered by (q,i)<(r,j) exactly when q<r; its two points a=(q,0), b=(q,1) at one level are incomparable, yet C({a})=C({b}) is the set of all other levels. This model satisfies the prose local/global directedness and divisibility axioms of scan 009, and its finite-chain gap partitions satisfy Dec₁–Dec₂, while no finite moderate set is maximal. Consequently the current axioms do not make T↦C(T) injective, even within the ordered model; the margin’s universal affirmative answer needs an additional separation hypothesis.',
 'S §1.1 raises but does not settle this issue; S §1.5 / SA21-R3-08 supplies the doubled-line model for a different question. E 119/34 emphasizes testing provisional axioms.', ['WG2 B15 (§1.2)','MPL_150_044-084 Q9'], 'targeted proof check','Explicit twin-singleton counterexample verified, including the applicable cut finiteness/refinement axioms; see research/math_checks.md. Refines the scan003 assertion.','High: both complements and all stated hypotheses have explicit descriptions; not claimed new in the literature.')
N(3,3,'“Connected components” are initially equivalence classes','unproved claim','data (b), last paragraph',
 'The classes of R_T are named “comp. connexes de C(T)” before a topology on L has been chosen. Their topological interpretation is therefore extra content, not a consequence of having an equivalence relation, and later interval examples over Q show why the distinction matters. The corresponding register warnings about component-only data are analogies, not proofs about this manuscript.',
 'E 119/12 uses actual cell-complement components; E 119/28–29 retains boundary and attachment maps. S §1.4 revisits the issue at scan019.', ['PATHS E.10','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'])
N(4,1,'Recovering boundary data and all cuts from two-point cuts','conjecture','“Je prévois” and numbered 1°, 2°',
 '“Je prévois que les axiomes impliquent” separates two expectations: frontier data (c) should follow from the partitions (b), and all partitions should follow from the two-point partitions. Scans 011–016 later give a recovery argument in the ordered model, but do not supply the full intrinsic axioms for arbitrary network data. The Esquisse’s explicit warning that some triple composition maps are not determined by adjacent pair data marks a limit on extending this special reconstruction pattern to general stratified objects.',
 'E 119/58–59, note (7), especially the NB about the undetermined long-edge map; S §2 lists the general axiom task as open.', ['EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e','STRATA B.1','MPL_150_044-084 N18','156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'])
N(4,2,'The displayed provisional axioms do not yet determine the frontier','own idea','data (c), compared with 005–006',
 'Let L be infinite, let every pair be moderate, and give each C(T)=L\\T one indiscrete equivalence class for every finite T. Both frontier assignments ∂C(T)=∅ and ∂C(T)=T satisfy ∂C(T)⊂T and Dec₂, and Dec₁ has one class; the finite/cofinite and maximal-finite premises on scan005 never arise. Hence the displayed axioms alone leave (c) underdetermined even when every R_T is fixed, identifying a missing requirement rather than refuting his prediction about a completed axiom system.',
 'E 119/34: provisional axioms may require additions after concrete tests; S §2 leaves the general (c)-from-(b) task open.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','S14 (packet README)'], 'targeted proof check','Two distinct frontier structures on identical pair and partition data checked against exactly the currently displayed axioms; detailed hypothesis boundary in research/math_checks.md.','High under the explicitly stated axiom list; a stronger unwritten intended axiom could exclude the example.')
N(5,1,'The discrete example is not a universal finiteness conclusion','unproved claim','Ex 1 and final Axiome',
 'The page specifies a discrete network and proposes that a maximal finite moderate T must be all of L, with the resulting structure discrete. This rules out finite maximal cuts in a nondiscrete infinite model, but does not assert that L itself is finite or that every complete moderate-pair graph has the discrete partition structure. The distinction matters for the countermodel in SP31-N004-02 and for any proposed finite reconstruction of an infinite form.',
 'E 119/32–34: local moderate models and provisional axioms; S §1.1 summarizes this discrete test.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','WG2 B15 (§1.2)'])
N(6,1,'Finiteness is a provisional choice of axioms','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','opening: finiteness hypotheses',
 'The qualification “peut-être provisoires” applies to the severe finiteness hypotheses introduced here. It is a reservation about which axiom system to adopt, not a missing proof of Dec₁, which is being imposed. This is closely parallel to the Esquisse’s admission that concrete tests may force extra or different axioms.',
 'E 119/34: “plausible provisoire”; S §1.1.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9'])
N(6,2,'Dec₂ gives a coherent finite-set refinement system','unpursued implication','Dec₁–Dec₂ and inclusion diagram',
 'For T⊂S, the unique containing component in Dec₂ defines a map from the finite class set of C(S) to that of C(T). For T⊂S⊂U, the direct map and the composite agree by the same uniqueness, so the written axiom already gives coherent transition maps on the inclusion category of moderate cuts. This is a genuine ingredient for S14, although neither cofilteredness of that category nor a geometric reconstruction theorem follows.',
 'E 119/36–37: a system indexed by flags with additional maps and structure; packet README S14.', ['WG2 B15 (§1.2)','STRATA A.4','S14 (packet README)'], 'targeted proof check','Transition-map composition follows from uniqueness of the containing R_T class, using nonempty equivalence classes; only this formal coherence checked.')
N(7,1,'The ordered example has finite cut pieces, not necessarily topologically connected pieces','unproved claim','Ex 2, component list and n+1 count',
 'The “n+1 composantes connexes” are the successive order gaps of a finite chain, with frontier labels recorded underneath. Density and absence of endpoints make those order pieces nonempty, but on Q the corresponding ordinary order-topological intervals are not connected. Read this as the model’s prescribed cut partition unless an additional topological connectedness hypothesis is given; the frozen final interval’s closing bracket and absent plus sign remain diplomatic readings.',
 'E 119/12 and119/28–29 distinguish actual geometric pieces; S §1.2, with the topological refinement in SA21-R3-06.', ['PATHS E.10','WG2 B15 (§1.2)'])
N(7,2,'The obvious finite-chain index is not generally cofiltered','own idea','finite chains as moderate cuts; compared with 010',
 'If u and v are incomparable, their singleton cuts have no common upper bound in the poset of finite moderate subsets, because a finite chain cannot contain both. Thus reversing the inclusion refinement order does not automatically give the cofiltered index required for the naive pro-object version of S14. A larger category of cuttings or a compatibility restriction might address this obstruction; the broad planning guess is not refuted.',
 'E 119/36–37; packet README S14; research/context_links.md CTX-OWN1.', ['WG2 B15 (§1.2)','STRATA A.4','S14 (packet README)'], 'targeted proof check','The missing common refinement is a direct consequence of finite moderation being the chain condition; no claim about every possible pro-object presentation.','High for this specific index obstruction; alternative indexing constructions remain open.')
N(8,1,'Order reversal loses orientation but preserves the cut data','unpursued implication','opening paragraph and interval cases',
 '“Elle ne dépend que du biordre” asserts invariance when the order is replaced by its opposite. The same comparable pairs and the same unlabeled interval pieces survive, while their left/right ordering is reversed; this explains why the later reconstruction aims at an order up to reversal. It is a statement about these data on L, not an isotopy or homotopy equivalence of the spaces envisioned in the Esquisse.',
 'E 119/33,36: comparison of categories and flag systems is stronger and different; S §1.2.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','MPL_121_001-048 Q4.3'])
N(8,2,'Finite unions introduce a second level of moderation','unproved claim','Partie modérée and final frontier sentence',
 'A moderate part is now a finite union of mutually compatible disjoint tronçons, and its boundary is declared to be the disjoint union of their boundaries. This advances beyond finite moderate subsets of lieux, so the two uses of moderate should not be conflated. S reports that 156-7/071 later renames the classes, but that retrospective terminology is context only and was not imported into the transcription.',
 'E 119/32–34 concerns axioms for moderate parts; S §1.2 and SA21-K3-01, citing later156-7/071.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','S14 (packet README)'])
N(9,1,'The order axioms do not force a linearly ordered or one-dimensional object','own idea','Definition and conditions a–d',
 'On R² define u≤ₛv to mean u=v or both coordinates of u are strictly smaller than those of v. This non-total order is directed both ways, divisible, and has directed nonempty strict cones, so it satisfies the prose tronçon conditions; its strict intervals are ordinary open rectangles. Thus those conditions alone admit a model with the Euclidean plane as canonical topology, a concrete limit on inferring one-dimensional geometry from the name tronçon.',
 'S §1.2 intends non-total examples, but its ordinary product-order example needs the distinction in SP31-N009-02; E 119/32–34 supplies only a broad axiomatic program.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','WG2 B15 (§1.2)','S14 (packet README)'], 'targeted proof check','Verified the explicitly different strong-coordinate order using coordinatewise bounds with positive gaps; interval basis identified. See research/math_checks.md.','High: all order axioms and the interval basis are directly checked; no novelty claim.')
N(9,2,'The sweep’s ordinary product-order example fails a written axiom','reading question','local directedness condition a; S §1.2',
 'S says that R² with the product order satisfies the tronçon axioms, but in the usual coordinatewise order x=(1,0) and y=(0,1) both lie strictly above a=(0,0) and have no common lower bound still strictly above a. This violates the displayed requirement that L_{>a} be downward directed. The strong-coordinate order of SP31-N009-01 is a separate valid model, not a silent reinterpretation of S’s ordinary product example.',
 'S §1.2 and STUDY’s appended156-3 discussion; SA21-R3-11 later uses ordinary product boxes outside the current image range.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9'], 'targeted proof check','Three-point coordinate obstruction refutes S’s example under the usual product order. Its outside-range consequence is not used as a verified counterexample here.')
N(9,3,'The written variables are not silently corrected in the mathematical reading','reading question','axiom a and dual a′',
 'The frozen text contains the literal “a>a” in the expanded a-clause and “a,y≤z<a” in the dual conclusion. The surrounding prose clearly states the local directedness requirements, but the written quantified expansions contain variable slips if read literally. The examples and proofs in these notes explicitly use those prose requirements; changing the formulas to the expected variables would be an editorial emendation.',
 'S §1.2 restates the prose conditions in repaired mathematical form; E has no tronçon definition.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'],outcome='Literal-versus-intended formula distinction recorded after freeze; no manuscript formula edited.')
N(10,1,'Flags of lieux are not the Esquisse’s enriched flag system','unpursued implication','S₀=Drap₂ and Modf=Drap displays',
 'The identification of moderate finite sets with flags makes the ordered example’s admissible cuts combinatorially explicit. In E119/36 the flags instead index spaces attached to strata, together with retractions, embeddings and fibrations. The common notation therefore supports a comparison of organization, not an identification of data or an automatic application of the finite-poset realization results.',
 'E119/36–37; S§1.2.', ['STRATA A.4','STRATA B.1','WG2 B15 (§1.2)'])
N(10,2,'A type mismatch in the final set-builder remains a reading question','reading question','last equality',
 'The final displayed comprehension literally has S⊂P_f(L), where the prose and neighboring flag formula suggest a finite subset S of L. S’s summary uses the mathematically coherent finite-chain interpretation, but that does not change the recorded subset sign. A future critical edition should distinguish a notational slip from a different intended domain rather than normalizing it inside this diplomatic text.',
 'S§1.2, “the finite tame parts are the finite chains”; E119/36 uses flags in a different domain.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(11,1,'The recovery assertion is fulfilled later in the ordered model','unproved claim','“Je dis que l’on récupère”',
 'The page asserts recovery of the biorder from data (a) and the pair-cut part of (b), and scans012–016 supply the intervening betweenness arguments. This supports the two Hua findings listed in RESULTS_STATE as claims about data already arising from a tronçon. It does not yet solve the converse realization problem for abstract pair relations and abstract partitions, which scan017 explicitly leaves to be formulated.',
 'RESULTS_STATE, final Hua findings section; S§1.3, SA21-R3-01 and03; E119/58–59 gives a structural contrast.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','156-3-betweenness-from-cuts','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'])
N(11,2,'Closed displayed intervals conflict with the complement definition','reading question','component list after the finite enumeration',
 'Several middle entries are written with closed brackets [s_i,s_{i+1}], although C(S) was defined to exclude every point of S. Read literally, those sets cannot be classes in a partition of C(S), because they contain the removed endpoints. S summarizes the cut pieces as open gaps, so the notes record the conflict without changing the visible brackets or assuming that the page meant closures.',
 'S§1.2 and§1.3; comparison with current003,007; E119/12 on actual complement components.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'], 'targeted proof check','Checked the set-theoretic incompatibility of included endpoints with C(S)⊂L∖S; suggested open-gap interpretation remains an editorial reading question.')
N(11,3,'The extreme-gap sentence also needs qualification','reading question','“les seules non vides éventuelles”',
 'The phrase “les seules non vides éventuelles” is followed by the lower ray when s₁ is a least element and the upper ray when s_n is a greatest element. Those rays are empty under precisely the parenthetic conditions, so the literal adjective and its explanation pull in opposite directions. The correct combinatorial count must treat empty extreme gaps separately, without repairing this phrase in the transcription.',
 'S§1.2 compresses the interval calculation; current010 identifies the extremities.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'], 'targeted proof check','A strict ray below a least element or above a greatest element is empty; the literal wording is not used as a nonemptiness theorem.')
N(12,1,'Strict betweenness determines comparability using density','unproved claim','Lemme and disjointness criterion',
 'The proof first recovers “a et b disjoints” from existence of a point strictly between them, explicitly invoking “divisibilité !” This isolates the hypothesis that turns the ternary relation into the binary comparability relation; the reconstruction claim is already registered by Hua and carried by SA21-R3-01. The present contribution is the precise manuscript route and its hypothesis, not a new claim of priority or a literature theorem.',
 'RESULTS_STATE finding156-3-order-from-betweenness; S§1.3/SA21-R3-01; E119/36 shares flag language but contains no proof of this order statement.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(12,2,'Strict and non-strict betweenness must remain separate','reading question','long NB with R and barred R',
 'The NB says that R “implique” the non-strict relation, then incorporates the small-cardinality flag cases in its reformulation. The printed opposed-arrow shape remains uncertain in the frozen text, and H’s stronger verb équivaut is not its literal wording. Any formal axiomatization should state separately which equality cases are being added to strict betweenness, rather than treating this graphic uncertainty as a theorem of equivalence.',
 'S§1.3 states the strict relation; E119/58–59 is a comparison of different low-arity coherence data.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'])
N(13,1,'A chosen oriented pair is the reconstruction seed','unproved claim','opening choice and criterion for u≤v',
 'Choosing an “origine” a in {a,b} fixes which of the two opposite orders is being recovered. The page describes the rays and middle interval from betweenness, then uses exterior x and y to test u≤v; directedness is what supplies common exterior bounds. SA21-R3-01 carries the reconstruction as checked in the earlier sweep, while this edition preserves the manuscript’s actual formulas and doubts instead of replacing them with that summary.',
 'S§1.3/SA21-R3-01; RESULTS_STATE finding156-3-order-from-betweenness; E119/36 provides only a structural comparison.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(13,2,'Recovery and intrinsic realization are different problems','own idea','orientation choice and four-condition test; compare017',
 'A useful next test is to separate recovery of an order from data known to come from one, from existence of an order realizing arbitrary proposed relations and pair partitions. The seed orientation and the four-point test suggest checking compatibility as the seed pair changes, but the manuscript does not prove that such local checks suffice. This could organize the open axiomatization request on017 without promoting the special recovery theorem into a general realization theorem.',
 'E119/58–59 distinguishes adjacent pair tubes from the extra triple composition map; research/context_links.md CTX-OWN2.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','156-3-betweenness-from-cuts','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'],confidence='Medium: the distinction is explicit in the manuscript, but sufficiency of any proposed compatibility test is unproved.')
N(14,1,'The finite lemma is a local proof step, not an open conjecture','unpursued implication','Lemme, rewritten proof, “OK” and NB',
 'The page reduces its comparison to a chain on “2, 3, ou 4” elements and ends the revised proof with “OK.” It therefore supplies a local proof step for the preceding reconstruction claim rather than merely restating an unresolved question; SA21-R3-02 records an earlier targeted check. The final NB distinguishes the roles of divisibility and two-sided directedness, which a later axiomatization should preserve.',
 'S§1.3/SA21-R3-02; RESULTS_STATE finding156-3-order-from-betweenness; E119/34 on testing proposed axioms.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(14,2,'“Trois” remains a textual slip, not an edited numeral','reading question','four variables x,y,u,v in the lemma',
 'The manuscript names x,y,u,v and writes “trois”; the ink-based edition retains that number. SA21-K3-02 explicitly proposed quatre from the mathematical count without checking this image, so its proposal belongs in commentary. The later allowance of 2,3,4 distinct values also shows why counting variable names is not itself an image-reading argument.',
 'S§1.3 and SA21-K3-02; no matching Esquisse passage.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(15,1,'Binary cut partitions can recover the ternary relation in the model','unproved claim','“On va voir maintenant” and Première condition',
 'The data here consist of comparable pairs and, for each pair, the unlabeled partition of its moderate complement; the triple must first be a flag. The reconstruction on016 then uses incidence of those parts as subsets of L, not merely the numbers or abstract isomorphism types of the parts. This is the exact scope of Hua’s156-3-betweenness-from-cuts finding and is weaker than reconstructing a stratified space from bare component counts.',
 'RESULTS_STATE finding156-3-betweenness-from-cuts; S§1.3/SA21-R3-03; E119/58–59 retains maps as part of reconstruction data.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e','MPL_150_044-084 N18'])
N(15,2,'The abandoned J-definition is a draft layer','withdrawn in the same text','cancelled final display015; replacement016',
 r'The definition beginning J_{abc}= is crossed out on015, while016 proceeds with three explicitly indexed components X_{\{a,b\}}, X_{\{b,c\}}, X_{\{c,a\}}. The cancelled expression is evidence of the route to the argument, not an additional live definition to reconcile with the later symbols. This also limits any reconstruction based on H’s presentation of that display as live text.',
 'S§1.3 summarizes the three-X formulation; E119/36 has no corresponding notation.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'])
N(16,1,'The middle element is characterized by disjoint incident-pair components','unpursued implication','three intersections, “divisibilité !”, “On gagne !”',
 'For a<b<c the page identifies the three pieces as L_{>b}, L_{<b} and ]a,c[, and exactly the two pieces indexed by pairs containing b are disjoint. The other two intersections are ]a,b[ and ]b,c[, whose nonemptiness is the explicit use of divisibility. SA21-R3-03 carries this special cut-partition argument as checked; it does not justify calling those pieces ordinary topological components in every model.',
 'S§1.3/SA21-R3-03; RESULTS_STATE finding156-3-betweenness-from-cuts; E119/58–59 gives a contrasting richer reconstruction.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts','PATHS E.10'])
N(16,2,'The final symbol and duplicate primes are preserved independently of the argument','reading question','final distinguishing-property paragraph and display',
 'The frozen page has apparent primes on both prose pair symbols and a pointed wedge in the final formula, whereas the three preceding tests use intersection signs. The component argument suggests two different incident pairs and an empty intersection, but that mathematical reconstruction is not a license to erase a prime or replace the wedge. Any formal restatement should display its notation changes as editorial interpretation.',
 'S§1.3 and Hua finding156-3-betweenness-from-cuts use the mathematically normalized intersection test.', ['156-3-betweenness-from-cuts'])
N(17,1,'The largest category exists under the nonempty-directed convention','unproved claim','Remarques 1, conditions a–c′ and conclusion',
 '“Il y a … plus grande … T” has a direct verification when directed posets are required to be nonempty. The intended tronçon class satisfies the conditions and is stable under nonempty strict cones; conversely the categorical conditions force global directions, divisibility and the two local cone directions. The replacement of non vide by cardinal ≠1 therefore needs the usual nonemptiness convention, rather than an unqualified claim that cardinal ≠1 by itself means cardinal ≥2.',
 'S§1.3/SA21-R3-04; E119/32–34 concerns proposed categories with axioms but does not establish this particular class.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','MPL_150_044-084 Q9'], 'targeted proof check','Checked stability under strict cones and opposite order and the role of the nonempty-directed convention; research/math_checks.md MC08.')
N(17,2,'Intrinsic axioms for both descriptions are explicitly left to be found','self-flagged: a step he himself marks as unverified','Remarques 2, final paragraph',
 '“Il faudrait expliciter” asks for conditions on R(a,b,c), and “itou” on S₀ and the pair-cut relations, that characterize biordered tronçons. The preceding recovery argument therefore does not yet constitute an equivalence with an intrinsically axiomatized data category. The Esquisse’s extra composition maps suggest a useful caution about local data, but are not themselves proposed tronçon axioms.',
 'S§2, first open task; E119/58–59, note(7), triple composition beyond adjacent pair data.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness','156-3-betweenness-from-cuts','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e','STRATA B.1'])
N(18,1,'The interval intersection statement can be proved using marked endpoints','unproved claim','intersection paragraph and oblique “argument topologique”',
 'The assertion that two compatible wide closed sub-tronçons intersect in another such piece, or in the empty set, follows by taking the larger lower bound and smaller upper bound among their chain-ordered endpoints. The new marked endpoints are among the old ones, proving the stated finite-frontier inclusion. This order proof does not need the proposed “argument topologique,” whose identification of marked endpoints with topological boundaries fails in general.',
 'S§1.4/SA21-R3-05; E119/35 warns that a boundary need not have its familiar manifold meaning; compare current023–025.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','PATHS E.10'], 'targeted proof check','Checked intervals, rays, absent bounds and singleton/empty outcomes; research/math_checks.md MC10. Only the marked-bound convention is used.')
N(18,2,'Marked endpoint data are not the canonical topological boundary','own idea','“frontière relative” and final “est la frontière”',
 'In the valid strong-coordinate model of SP31-N009-01, [a,b] for a=(0,0), b=(1,1) is the open unit square together with its two opposite corners. Its marked endpoint set has two points, but its closure is the closed square and its topological boundary is the full perimeter; even in an ordinary bounded chain the ambient extremities require a relative-boundary convention. Thus the blanket identification on018 cannot justify the finite-frontier claims, and the later retreat on023–025 is mathematically substantive.',
 'E119/35 and58 distinguishes geometric boundary data from an unjustified identification; S§1.5 notices nonclosed segments but does not supply this model.', ['MPL_150_044-084 Q9','STRATA A.4'], 'targeted proof check','Computed the interval, its Euclidean closure/interior and marked endpoints in an admissible tronçon. Relative-boundary convention explicitly distinguished.','High: the two sets are computed directly in the verified model; an illustrative check, not a novelty claim.')
N(18,3,'The boundary variable on the upper ray remains literal','reading question','definition of closed sub-tronçon and inserted frontier list',
 'The upper ray is written with subscript a after a canceled b, while its exception and frontier entry still use b. The consistent mathematical formulation would use one endpoint throughout, but this is an unpropagated correction in the written definition. The research arguments use a named ray endpoint explicitly and do not silently treat the conflicting letters as a new convention.',
 'S§1.4 gives a normalized list of closed rays; E has no corresponding tronçon formula.', ['156-3-order-from-betweenness'])
N(19,1,'The topological connected-component interpretation is false over Q','reading question','Th 1°, bracket “les comp. conn. de U”',
 'The literal bracket identifies the pieces Tᵢ with connected components, yet in L=Q the single order interval [0,1]∩Q is separated by every irrational cut between two of its points and has singleton topological components. This verifies the need for S’s correction of the interpretation, without changing the bracket or its residual U in the transcription. S’s later quotation from030 is context only; completeness has not been formalized here for all partial orders, so no blanket positive converse is asserted.',
 'S§1.4, SA21-K3-06/SA21-R3-06, reporting later156-3/030; E119/12,28–29 uses genuine geometric components.', ['PATHS E.10','EXIT_ONE_TYPE P4e'], 'targeted proof check','Irrational-cut separation in Q checked. Refutes the topological reading, not the separate uniqueness of the prescribed interval decomposition.')
N(19,2,'Uniqueness and boundary union need a combinatorial formulation','unproved claim','Th 1°–2°, definition and margin X⊂Y',
 'The page states uniqueness of the finite disjoint interval decomposition, the union formula for its finite boundary, and the implication X⊂Y ⇒ relative moderation. Those are claims about pieces with compatible marked endpoints, independently of ordinary topological connectedness. The required proof uses divisibility to prevent a nondegenerate interval from being split into finitely many separated closed pieces and uses the ordered endpoints to compare an inner piece with the boundary of an outer piece.',
 'S§1.4 carries the decomposition argument; E119/28–30 additionally retains attachment information, which this uniqueness statement does not provide.', ['PATHS E.10','MPL_150_044-084 Q9'])
